Z||000000||drednot||04-11-2001||07:12 PM||||Ok, I am at the point where I just want to see if the system will work! How do I bypass everything and get the compressor clutch to come on. The compressor has 2 wires running into it. what do I do to get it to turn on? I am considering running a switch from my dash to the compressor clutch so I can cycle it on and off manually. thoughts? suggestions??||||reg||9
Z||000001||rogergarrison||04-11-2001||07:23 PM||||the clutch is supposed to cycle itself when you push the buttons on for A/c. Why do you need to switch it on and off seperately, am I missing something?||||reg||1
Z||000002||drednot||04-11-2001||07:59 PM||||yes, the car's a/c controls were damaged by accident and no longer work.  I need some way to bypass them and cycle the compressor on and off manualy.  All I need to know is how to do that.||||reg||8
Z||000003||1988LRC||04-11-2001||08:01 PM||||put a jumper wire between the connections of the low pressure switch its the one in the accumulator||||reg||1
Z||000004||drednot||04-11-2001||08:04 PM||||that's the thing, They blew out.  I have to go directly to the compressor.||||reg||1
Z||000005||84Bill||04-11-2001||08:15 PM||||if you run a jumper from the battery to the one terminal on the clutch pin (nearest the clutch) and the other (pin) to ground the clutch should engage<BR>shame on you if you attempt this while the engine is running AND you do realize you are bypassing ALL the SAFTEY systems<P>black is ground and the dark green is the positive that is according to the crappy haynes manual I have.. dont ever buy the crappy haynes manual ... it sucks<P>oh and I don't recomend using bailing wire unless its insulated with bubble gum!<p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 04-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Indiana_resto_guy||04-11-2001||08:20 PM||||What was damaged in prior accident?<BR>Which, by the way, you failed to mention in your previous post. I find it to be a lot of guess work in providing any help with incomplete info. Depending of what was damaged, your A/C may not even work no matter how much bubble gum and bailing wire you wish to put on it!<BR>Come clean!||||reg||1
Z||000007||84Bill||04-11-2001||08:34 PM||||Here is more info than you prolly need<BR>there are many switches and relays in the system cars with the vin R have a high and low press switches  and a compressor control relay as well as a solid state control module <P>Vin 9 cars have a Press cycling switch that opens at 24psi and closes at 48 psi and an A/C cutout switch that pops at 425 psi!<BR>as well as th compressor control relay and solid state control mod||||reg||1
Z||000008||drednot||04-12-2001||09:03 AM||||I did it, I wirwed up a toggel switch under the dash.  I HAVE A.C.!  All I have to watch for now is freez ups.  Not a big problem though.||||reg||6
Z||000009||84Bill||04-12-2001||09:45 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by drednot:<BR><B>I did it, I wirwed up a toggel switch under the dash.  I HAVE A.C.!  All I have to watch for now is freez ups.  Not a big problem though.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>You also know when you wire it up this way you will need to be sure your coolant fan is coming on! else you'll be nice and cool while your poor engine melts<BR>||||reg||1
